{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"caption\":\"United States v. Hunt\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Tenth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2023-03-29\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"63 F.4th 1229\",\"proposition\":\"A categorical challenge to firearm-toolmark identification does not determine admissibility for every case; Rule 702 reliability remains a case-specific inquiry committed to the district court’s gatekeeping discretion. Later circuits have treated Hunt as a leading response to modern scientific criticism of toolmark evidence.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"forensic evidence / firearm-toolmark identification / Rule 702\",\"discovery_path\":[\"https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/21-4231/21-4231-2024-04-16.html\"],\"confidence\":\"medium\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Juniper v. Davis\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2023-07-19\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"74 F.4th 196; No. 21-9\",\"proposition\":\"Adopted a two-stage cumulative-materiality framework for mixed Brady and Napue claims: first aggregate the Napue evidence with Brady evidence tending to show falsity under Napue’s more defense-favorable standard; then aggregate all Brady and Napue evidence under Brady’s materiality standard.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Brady/Napue / cumulative materiality\",\"discovery_path\":[\"https://www.ca4.uscourts.gov/opinions/219.P.pdf\"],\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Daniels\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Eleventh Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-03-29\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 22-13590\",\"proposition\":\"Reaffirmed the Eleventh Circuit’s disfavor toward eyewitness-reliability experts and upheld exclusion after an individualized Rule 702 helpfulness review, expressly contrasting the more receptive Second and Sixth Circuits. It also upheld the identification under the Biggers/Manson framework and permitted familiar-officer video-identification testimony under Rule 701.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"eyewitness identification / expert testimony / circuit split\",\"discovery_path\":[\"https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca11/22-13590/22-13590-2024-03-29.html\"],\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Hunt\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-04-16\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"99 F.4th 161; No. 21-4231\",\"proposition\":\"Historic admission of forensic evidence does not discharge Rule 702 gatekeeping, especially when modern science questions the method. Nevertheless, the court upheld admission of firearm-toolmark testimony where the trial court limited overstatement of accuracy and allowed extensive confrontation; it declined to declare the discipline categorically admissible or inadmissible.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"forensic evidence / firearm-toolmark identification / Rule 702\",\"discovery_path\":[\"https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/21-4231/21-4231-2024-04-16.html\"],\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Johnson v. City of Cheyenne\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Tenth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-04-26\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"99 F.4th 1206; No. 22-8015\",\"proposition\":\"A one-photo identification is not per se unconstitutional; reliability controls, and a witness’s prior familiarity with the suspect may strongly support reliability beyond the enumerated Biggers factors. The court also rejected Brady and Trombetta/Youngblood claims involving speculative missing photographs and reiterated that civil liability for investigators requires knowing or reckless conduct rather than negligence.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"eyewitness identification / single-photo procedure / Brady and evidence preservation\",\"discovery_path\":[\"https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca10/22-8015/22-8015-2024-04-26.html\"],\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Smith v. Arizona\",\"court\":\"U.S. Supreme Court\",\"date\":\"2024-06-21\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"602 U.S. 779; No. 22-899\",\"proposition\":\"When an expert conveys an absent analyst’s statements to support an opinion, and those statements support the opinion only if true, they are admitted for their truth. If the statements are also testimonial, the Confrontation Clause bars them absent unavailability and a prior opportunity for cross-examination; the Court left the testimonial/primary-purpose determination for remand.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"forensic evidence / substitute analysts / Confrontation Clause\",\"discovery_path\":[\"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/23pdf/22-899_97be.pdf\"],\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Ramsey v. Rivard\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-08-01\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"110 F.4th 860; No. 23-1941\",\"proposition\":\"Denied qualified immunity where a jury could find a police-arranged show-up unnecessarily suggestive, unnecessary, and unreliable. Consulting a prosecutor, possessing other circumstantial evidence, or having the prosecutor proceed does not shield an officer; the constitutional injury is use of the impermissible identification leading to conviction.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"eyewitness identification / §1983 officer liability / circuit split\",\"discovery_path\":[\"https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca6/23-1941/23-1941-2024-08-01.html\"],\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Gallegos\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Tenth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-08-05\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-2010\",\"proposition\":\"Although the police show-up was undisputedly suggestive, the identification was admissible because a holistic totality analysis—including, but not limited to, the Biggers factors—showed no very substantial likelihood of irreparable misidentification. The court acknowledged modern research concerning stress, weapon focus, and the weak correlation between confidence and accuracy.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"eyewitness identification / suggestive show-ups / reliability\",\"discovery_path\":[\"https://www.ca10.uscourts.gov/sites/ca10/files/opinions/010111089945.pdf\"],\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Rega v. Secretary, Pennsylvania Department of Corrections\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Third Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-08-23\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"115 F.4th 235; Nos. 18-9002 and 18-9003\",\"proposition\":\"Restated the distinct Brady and Giglio/Napue tests in a capital habeas case and applied AEDPA deference. The court declined to decide whether separate Brady and Giglio/Napue claims must always be assessed cumulatively, preserving that question in the Third Circuit.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Brady/Giglio/Napue / materiality / unresolved cumulative-analysis question\",\"discovery_path\":[\"https://www.leagle.com/decision/infco20240823089\"],\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Ruiz\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Tenth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-09-16\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"116 F.4th 1246; No. 23-2027\",\"proposition\":\"A court may reject a due-process identification challenge on reliability grounds without definitively deciding whether the procedure was impermissibly suggestive. A concurrence urged courts to make explicit suggestiveness findings to guide police practices.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"eyewitness identification / sequencing of Manson-Biggers analysis\",\"discovery_path\":[\"https://app.midpage.ai/case/united-states-v-ruiz-10585203\"],\"confidence\":\"medium\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Martin v. Nines\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-01-16\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-6086\",\"proposition\":\"The divided panel held that a state court unreasonably applied Brady materiality by treating strong remaining evidence as dispositive and failing to assess how a suppressed forensic-computer report changed the character of the entire case. This judgment was reversed by Klein v. Martin and is no longer controlling.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Brady materiality / AEDPA / subsequently reversed\",\"discovery_path\":[\"https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/24-6086/24-6086-2025-01-16.html\",\"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/607/25-51/\"],\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Glossip v. Oklahoma\",\"court\":\"U.S. Supreme Court\",\"date\":\"2025-02-25\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"145 S. Ct. 612; No. 22-7466\",\"proposition\":\"The prosecution knowingly failed to correct false testimony and violated Napue. The duty to correct rests on the State, regardless of what the defense knew or should have known; the question is whether the failure to correct could in any reasonable likelihood have affected the jury, ordinarily placing on the State the burden to establish harmlessness beyond a reasonable doubt. The false statement need not itself directly determine the verdict.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Napue / knowing false testimony / materiality and harmlessness\",\"discovery_path\":[\"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/24pdf/22-7466_5h25.pdf\"],\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Green v. Thomas\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-03-03\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"129 F.4th 877; No. 24-60314\",\"proposition\":\"A complaint plausibly alleged clearly established Fourteenth Amendment violations where a detective allegedly redirected an informant from the person initially selected in a photo array to the plaintiff and withheld information undermining both the informant and probable cause. A grand-jury indictment did not break causation where material facts allegedly were withheld.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"eyewitness identification / manipulated lineup / Brady-derived officer liability\",\"discovery_path\":[\"https://caselaw.findlaw.com/court/us-5th-circuit/117019264.html\"],\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Clark v. Louisville-Jefferson County Metropolitan Government\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-03-07\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"130 F.4th 571; No. 24-5061\",\"proposition\":\"The Sixth Circuit’s Brady-derived disclosure duty applies to police and forensic scientists when evidence’s exculpatory value should be apparent; bad faith is unnecessary. A forensic serologist’s compliance with a laboratory policy disclosing only final reports did not defeat potential §1983 liability for withholding conflicting bench notes, creating or deepening a circuit split over the mental state required for officer or analyst liability.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Brady / forensic bench notes / §1983 mental-state split\",\"discovery_path\":[\"https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/USCOURTS-ca6-24-05061/pdf/USCOURTS-ca6-24-05061-0.pdf\"],\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Blackmon v. Jones\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Seventh Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-03-20\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"132 F.4th 522\",\"proposition\":\"Took the opposing side of the §1983 identification split: police are not liable merely because prosecutors offered and judges admitted suggestive identification evidence at trial. Absent police deception, pressure on the prosecution or court, or another causal basis, the trial actors’ decisions prevent attribution of the fair-trial violation to the officer.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"eyewitness identification / §1983 causation / circuit split\",\"discovery_path\":[\"https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/25/25-378/377967/20250926093113524_25-%20Petition.pdf\"],\"confidence\":\"medium-high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Salter v. City of Detroit\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-03-21\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"133 F.4th 527; No. 22-1656\",\"proposition\":\"Denied qualified immunity for a single-mugshot identification that was admitted at trial and lacked adequate reliability; the prosecutor’s use of the identification did not shield the detective. The court also adhered to Sixth Circuit precedent allowing Brady-derived officer liability without bad faith. Separate opinions questioned both rules under recent Supreme Court precedent, sharply exposing splits with the Second, Seventh, Eighth, and Tenth Circuits on identifications and with most circuits on Brady mental state.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"eyewitness identification and Brady / §1983 circuit splits\",\"discovery_path\":[\"https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/25/25-378/377967/20250926093113524_25-%20Petition.pdf\"],\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Seward\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-04-25\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-4431\",\"proposition\":\"Applied Smith v. Arizona to DNA testimony and abrogated United States v. Summers. A reviewing expert cannot evade confrontation by offering an ostensibly independent opinion that necessarily accepts a non-testifying analyst’s case-specific laboratory work as true; generic testimony about laboratory procedures and quality controls remains permissible. The testimonial-primary-purpose issue remained for further analysis.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"forensic DNA / substitute analysts / Confrontation Clause\",\"discovery_path\":[\"https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/23-4431/23-4431-2025-04-25.html\"],\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Johnson v. Superintendent Mahanoy SCI\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Third Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-07-14\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-2531; amended orders/opinions issued 2025-07-30\",\"proposition\":\"Newly specified the defendant’s burden on Brady suppression when possession is disputed: the habeas petitioner must produce credible evidence supporting a reasonable inference that the prosecution possessed the alleged Brady material and failed to disclose it. A witness’s unsupported, belated, and unreliable accusation that information was given to police may be insufficient.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Brady / proof of prosecution possession and suppression\",\"discovery_path\":[\"https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca3/23-2531/23-2531-2025-07-14.html\"],\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Moore v. State of Maryland\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2026-01-07\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-6325\",\"proposition\":\"Held that mid-trial disclosure can constitute Brady suppression when the delay prevents effective investigation, preparation, and presentation. A late-produced chain-of-custody report was favorable and material because timely disclosure could have supported an alternative defense and impeachment of proof essential to identifying the alleged controlled substance.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Brady / delayed disclosure / effective use at trial\",\"discovery_path\":[\"https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/24-6325/24-6325-2026-01-07.html\"],\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Klein v. Martin\",\"court\":\"U.S. Supreme Court\",\"date\":\"2026-01-26\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"607 U.S. ___; No. 25-51\",\"proposition\":\"Summarily reversed the Fourth Circuit’s grant of Brady habeas relief. Under AEDPA, a state court receives the benefit of the doubt and need not discuss every item of evidence or write a sufficiently nuanced opinion; even evidence that entirely discredits a prosecution witness may reasonably be found immaterial when considerable independent evidence strongly supports conviction.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Brady materiality / AEDPA deference / reversal of Martin v. Nines\",\"discovery_path\":[\"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/607/25-51/\"],\"confidence\":\"high\"}"}}
